[3] at the physical coupling beta = 2.5 (the point the charter cares about)
    m_lat = 0.329420 ,  m_tree(cav=7) = 0.000000  (ordered: cav*ratio = 5.0354 > 1)
    m_tree(cav=3) = 0.000000    =>  the tree bound m_lat >= m_tree is 0 : VACUOUS
    (the bound is non-trivial only for beta < beta_c^tree = 0.2897, i.e. deep
     strong coupling, where the tree value DIVERGES as beta -> 0 and is useless near 2.5)

[4] small-beta check (strong coupling): m_lat + log(beta/2) -> 0 , m_tree - m_lat -> log(cav)
   beta   m_lat+log(b/2)   m_tree7-m_lat     log7
  0.050     4.164931e-04       -1.945910   1.9459
  0.100     1.663895e-03       -1.945910   1.9459
  0.200     6.622584e-03       -1.945910   1.9459

[5] the tree bound  B(beta) := max(0, m_tree(beta))  vs beta
    B > 0 only for beta < beta_c^tree;  B = 0 for every beta above it,
    in particular for the whole scaling window beta in [2.0, 3.0].

[6] reading
    (i)  in the window where the tree is disordered, m_tree < m_lat ALWAYS, with the
         beta-independent shift  m_lat - m_tree = log(cav)  (here log 7 = 1.9459);
    (ii) the shift is exactly the connectivity multiplicity, i.e. the tree is again
         the 'worst case' (fast counting, slow decay, small mass);
    (iii) at beta_c^tree the tree's own gap closes; above it the tree bound is 0,
         so at beta = 2.5 the comparison yields  m_lat >= 0  : vacuous.
